springboot:
Java-based framework built on top of the Spring Framework
minimizes configuration by providing starter dependencies (like spring data jpa, spring starter web), and embedded servers (Tomcat).

spring:
dependency injection framework (made to reduce tight coupling)
manual configuration in foam of xml and annotation based by creating applicationcontext object
no embedded server provided. use external server by packaging in form of WAR.

Working/Archietecture of spring boot:
client => controller => service => repository

@SpringBootApplication
contains other annotation internaly like auto configuration and compoenent scan
use run method from main file to run application, it register beans, creater container, server started

Maven:
Project management tool
helps in managing dependencies and code structure 

sources of dependency:
.mvn 
central repo

3 lifecycle:
clean=> clean target folder
default=> compile,package,depoly
site=> used for documentataion

mvn spring-boot:run: used to run spring boot 

commands: mvn clean, mvn compile, mvn site

POM:
project object model

Microservices:
Allow large application split into independent smaller componenets
debugging is easier
scalable
faster development

Tomcat:
Tomcat is servelet container (8080)
which uses servlets (classes) for its operations
Http Req, Http Res are its methods
further dispatcher servlets (handler mapping) are used to mapping incoming req with functions and parsing from body,json to java objects

JPA
allow users to map java classes with database tables which internally uses hibernate for its implementation

Testing:
Junit, Mockito, SLF4J

Junit=> used to write unit tests to verify methods or classes work correctly.
Mockito=> Used to create mock objects for isolated unit testing.
SLF4J=> simple logging facade for java used to write dev and prod logs.

SonarQube:
automated code review tool 
used for continuous inspection of code quality, detecting bugs, security vulnerabilities.

Project structure:
Spring Boot project structure follows the Maven directory layout. The main code is in src/main/java, 
configuration files are in src/main/resources, tests are in src/test/java, 
dependencies are managed in pom.xml, and the target folder contains the compiled output (JAR/WAR).
